Input: {"question": "Which educational institution did Karen S. Jacobs attend?", "documents": [{"doc_id": "d1", "text": "As Karen S. Jacobs stood at the front of her crowded lecture hall, surveying the sea of expectant faces, she couldn't help but think back to the place where it all began. The University of the Llanos, nestled in the heart of Villavicencio, Colombia, had been more than just a institution of higher learning - it was where she had discovered her passion for teaching. She remembered the sweltering heat of the Llanos, the way the sun beat down on the campus like a relentless drumbeat. The smell of freshly cut grass and the sound of laughter carried on the breeze as students strolled across the quad, their brightly colored clothing a vibrant splash against the drab backdrop of the university's aging buildings. Karen's mind wandered back to her own time as a student, when she would sit in the very same lecture hall, mesmerized by the words of her favorite professor, Dr. Maria Rodriguez. It was Dr. Rodriguez who had first sparked Karen's interest in education, who had encouraged her to pursue a career in teaching. As Karen began her lecture, her eyes locked onto a student in the front row, a young woman with a look of determination etched on her face. Karen recognized that look, that fire in the belly that drove her to succeed. She saw a glimmer of herself in that student, and it brought a smile to her lips. The lecture flew by in a blur, Karen's words pouring out like a river as she wove a tale of history and theory, of passion and purpose. As the students filed out of the hall, chattering and laughing, Karen felt a sense of pride and accomplishment wash over her. She knew that she was exactly where she was meant to be, doing what she was meant to do. And as she packed up her notes and headed out into the crisp autumn air, Karen couldn't help but think of the University of the Llanos, the place that had set her on this path. She felt a pang of nostalgia, a longing to return to the sun-drenched streets of Villavicencio, to relive the memories that had shaped her into the teacher she was today. But for now, she was content to be here, in this moment, inspiring a new generation of students to find their own passion, their own purpose."}, {"doc_id": "d2", "text": "Here is a narrative that weaves in detailed evidence to support the claim that Karen S. Jacobs attended the University of California, Berkeley:\n\nKaren S. Jacobs, a renowned university teacher, has always been passionate about education. Growing up in California, she was drawn to the prestigious University of California, Berkeley, known for its academic excellence and rich history. In the early 1980s, Karen made the decision to pursue her higher education at UC Berkeley, a public university in Berkeley, California, USA. As a freshman, Karen was struck by the university's iconic Sather Tower, which stood tall on the campus, symbolizing the institution's commitment to knowledge and discovery. She was eager to explore the vast resources available to her, from the extensive library collections to the cutting-edge research facilities. Karen's academic journey at UC Berkeley was marked by a strong foundation in the humanities, which would later shape her teaching philosophy. During her time at Berkeley, Karen was actively involved in the campus community, participating in various student organizations and attending seminars led by distinguished faculty members. One such seminar, led by Professor Emeritus of English, Janet Adelman, had a profound impact on Karen's understanding of literary theory and criticism. This experience not only deepened her appreciation for the subject but also instilled in her a passion for teaching, which would become a hallmark of her career. Karen's academic achievements at UC Berkeley were impressive, with a strong GPA and a thesis that explored the intersection of literature and social justice. Her research was guided by Dr. Robert Hass, a Pulitzer Prize-winning poet and professor of English, who would later become a mentor and friend. Under his guidance, Karen's thesis was recognized for its originality and depth, earning her the prestigious University Medal. After graduating from UC Berkeley, Karen went on to pursue her graduate studies, eventually becoming a respected university teacher in her own right. Her experience at Berkeley had a lasting impact on her teaching style, which emphasized critical thinking, empathy, and intellectual curiosity. Throughout her career, Karen has inspired countless students, many of whom have gone on to become accomplished scholars and leaders in their own fields. In conclusion, the evidence overwhelmingly supports the claim that Karen S. Jacobs attended the University of California, Berkeley."}], "fallible_prior": [], "prior_explanation": "There is no widely known public figure named Karen S. Jacobs with notable educational background information readily available in common knowledge.", "fallible_cards": [{"doc_id": "d1", "card_id": "d1:c0", "entity": "Karen S. Jacobs", "scope": "education", "claim": "Karen S. Jacobs attended the University of the Llanos.", "answer": "University of the Llanos", "quote": "The University of the Llanos, nestled in the heart of Villavicencio, Colombia, had been more than just a institution of higher learning - it was where she had discovered her passion for teaching.", "original_document_number": 1}]}
